abstract: >-
  Two Lean 4 proofs of the class number one theorem for imaginary quadratic fields, each accepted by two independent kernels
  (Lean's and nanoda) through Comparator in local, unsandboxed runs: for squarefree m >= 1, the field Q(sqrt(-m)) has class
  number one if and only if m is in {1, 2, 3, 7, 11, 19, 43, 67, 163}. Neither uses Heegner, Baker, Stark or Gross-Zagier input,
  and both prove the same Lean statement. The first goes through OpenAI's zero-free region Re s > 7/8 for Dirichlet L-functions,
  whose Lean proof is inside the checked closure, and the Chowla-Selberg formula at s = 7/8, which gives the explicit bound
  d <= 652 proved in Lean, followed by kernel-checked split-prime witnesses for the finite range. The second, the determinant
  route, has no L-function, no zeta function and no zero-free theorem in its closure and needs only Mathlib: Green's derivative
  determinant, made nonzero by van Dijk's finite-field argument and divisible at every inert prime by Frobenius, rules out
  discriminants q >= 2^15 under class number one, and a kernel-checked residue sieve settles the range below. Written for
  T. Tao's tenth-discriminant challenge (20 October 2026).
